    The cohort was highly experienced with antiretroviral therapy, with a mean (SD) duration of treatment of 8.82 (4.00) years. The median duration was 11.05 years (IQR: 6.20, 11.80), ranging from 0.4 to 20.2 years. Half of the participants (307, 50.5%) had been on ART for 11 years or longer, while a small minority (58, 9.5%) had been on treatment for one year or less.

The majority of participants (335, 55.0%) had a clinic refill visit frequency of 1-3 months, followed closely by a sizeable proportion (239, 39.2%) who had refill visits less frequently (greater than 6 months). A small group (35, 5.7%) had refills every 4-6 months. No participants reported weekly refill visits.

Adherence to medication was generally high. A large majority of participants (498, 81.8%) reported missing no doses in the previous 30 days. However, 111 participants (18.2%) reported some level of non-adherence. Of these, 71 (11.7% of total) reported missing more than five doses, indicating a significant adherence challenge in a subset of the cohort. This is further reflected in the pattern of intake: while most participants (473, 77.7%) reported taking their medication exactly as prescribed, 130 (21.3%) reported sometimes taking it on time and sometimes late, and 6 (1.0%) reported often forgetting or skipping doses.

The vast majority of participants (592, 97.2%) reported never having discontinued their ART for more than one consecutive month. Concurrent use of herbal mixtures for HIV or other illnesses was low, with only 33 participants (5.4%) reporting use.

Among the 111 participants who missed doses, the most frequently cited reasons were forgetfulness (64, 57.7%) and travel or mobility challenges (49, 44.1%). Stigma or discrimination was a factor for 28 participants (25.2%), and side effects were cited by 13 (11.7%). A small number reported discontinuing due to feeling better (4, 3.6%) or lack of access to medication (4, 3.6%).


