At the initiation of anti-retroviral therapy, the entire study cohort (609 participants, 100.0%) was classified under WHO Clinical Stage 1, indicating that all participants were in the least severe stage of HIV disease at baseline. At the current assessment, the overwhelming majority of participants (603, 99.0%) remained in or were classified as WHO Clinical Stage 1. This indicates a sustained state of clinical well-being and immune health for most of the cohort following ART initiation. A very small number of participants (6, 1.0%) experienced disease progression. Three participants (0.5%) progressed to Stage 2, and another three (0.5%) progressed to the more severe Stage 4. The absence of any participants in Stage 3 at the current assessment is noted.
  
    
Anthropometric and Virological Outcomes:
Participants demonstrated stable or improved health metrics since initiating ART. The mean weight increased slightly from 67.34 kg at baseline to a current average weight of 68.37 kg, while the median age remained stable between baseline and current measurements at 65.0 kg (IQR: 57.0, 75.0) - baseline and 65.0 kg (IQR: 58.0, 76.0) - current assessment. The median weight change was 0.0 kg (IQR: 0.0, 0.0), with a mean increase of 1.03 kg (SD 3.58). The majority of participants (406, 66.7%) experienced no change in weight, while 152 (25.0%) gained weight and 51 (8.4%) experienced weight loss.

Virologically, the cohort showed excellent suppression. The median viral load remained stable at 19.00 copies/ml from ART commencement to the current time point. Crucially, 96.9% (n=590) of participants achieved the primary outcome of clinical viral load suppression (<1000 copies/ml). Only 19 participants (3.1%) had unsuppressed viral loads (≥1000 copies/ml). This objective measure is supported by self-reported data, where 97.9% of participants (sum of 'Undetectable' and 'Detectable but below 1000') believed their last result indicated virological success. It is important to note that most participants (328, 53.9%) had their last viral load test more than a year ago.

History of Opportunistic Infections and Comorbidities:
The vast majority of participants (479, 78.8%) reported no history of opportunistic infections or related symptoms in the preceding six months. The most commonly reported symptom was weight loss (120, 19.7%), followed by cough (23, 3.8%). Other symptoms, such as night sweats, difficulty breathing, pain while urinating, and new comorbidities, were rare (each <1%). No cases of oral thrush or sexually transmitted infection symptoms (genital sores, vaginal discharge) were reported, indicating effective immune reconstitution.

Self-Assessed Health Progress:
An overwhelming majority of participants (607, 99.7%) reported an improvement in their overall health since commencing ART, with 461 (75.7%) reporting feeling "much better" and 146 (24.0%) feeling "slightly better." Only two participants (0.3%) reported no change, and none reported their health had worsened.  
  
  


