    This analysis compared self-reported adherence behaviors against the gold standard of clinician-documented adherence categorization. The distribution of clinician-documented adherence was heavily skewed, with the overwhelming majority of participants (598, 98.2%) classified as having good adherence (≥95%). A small number were classified as having fair adherence (8, 1.3%) and only three participants (0.5%) were classified in the poor adherence category.

Pattern of ART Intake:
A Fisher’s Exact test revealed no statistically significant association between clinician-documented adherence and self-reported pattern of medication intake (p=0.4). While the numbers in the poor and fair adherence groups are small, a trend is observable. A higher proportion of participants in the poor (33.3%) and fair (37.5%) adherence groups reported a suboptimal pattern of intake ("sometimes on time, sometimes late") compared to those in the good adherence group (21.1%). All participants who reported the worst pattern ("often forget or skip doses") were, as expected, categorized in the good adherence group by clinicians, which may indicate a discrepancy in reporting versus assessment.

Doses Missed in the Last 30 Days:
Similarly, there was no statistically significant association between the number of missed doses in the last month and clinician-documented adherence (p=0.3). Again, a trend suggests that participants with poorer clinician-documented adherence were more likely to report missing doses. For instance, 33.3% of the poor adherence group and 25.0% of the fair adherence group reported missing more than five doses, compared to 11.4% of the good adherence group. Conversely, a lower percentage of participants in the poor (66.7%) and fair (62.5%) adherence groups reported missing zero doses compared to the good adherence group (82.1%).

Treatment Discontinuation:
There was no significant association between clinician-documented adherence and having ever discontinued ART for more than one month (p>0.9). All participants in the poor and fair adherence groups reported never having discontinued treatment. The 17 participants who reported a treatment interruption were all categorized in the good adherence group by clinicians, highlighting a potential limitation of a single clinician assessment to capture historical adherence behavior.  
  
  

