The most frequently reported enabler was the use of reminder systems, cited by 437 participants (71.8%). This included tools such as phone alarms, SMS reminders, phone calls, and pill organizers. The second most common enabler was support from family and friends, reported by 315 participants (51.7%). Other enablers, such as easy access to medication (3.6%), health worker support (1.3%), and an understanding of adherence importance (0.7%), were reported by a small minority of participants.

Barriers to Adherence:
A strikingly high number of participants, 507 (83.3%), reported stigma and discrimination as a barrier to adherence. This was the most prominent challenge by a large margin. The next most common barriers were long distance to the health facility (110, 18.1%) and side effects of ART (60, 9.9%). Patient-specific factors like forgetfulness (33, 5.4%) and lack of transport fare (11, 1.8%) were less frequently cited. Notably, no participants cited a poor attitude of health workers as a barrier.



